<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pergamon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pergamon was an ancient Greek city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, near modern Bergama in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and the capital of the Attalid kingdom. It became one of the great centres of Hellenistic culture, with a famous library and the Great Altar. It was among the cities that flourished as centres of commerce and scholarship in the world created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pergamon.jpg" alt="Pergamon" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Jones from Kelowna, BC, Canada · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acropolis_-_Bergama_(Pergamon)_-_Turkey_-_10_(5747249729).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Philip V (221–179 BCE) began energetically, winning the Social War against the Aetolian League (220–217 BCE), but his alliance with Hannibal made Rome an enemy, and his aggressive campaigns in the Aegean after 202 BCE alarmed Rhodes and <b>Pergamon</b>, which appealed to the Senate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The tribute paid to the Gauls of Tylis had strained its finances in the third century BCE, and the rise of Bithynia and <b>Pergamon</b> limited its room to manoeuvre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Apamea in 188 BCE gave it to the Kingdom of <b>Pergamon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…in the temple of Sabazios at Gordium in 333 BCE; the Celts overran the north, the Attalids of <b>Pergamon</b> took the southern remnant in 188 BCE and Rome inherited it in 133 BCE, splitting it between the provinces of Galatia and Asia and later, under Diocletian, into Phrygia…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexandria, and later successor foundations such as Antioch along with older cities such as <b>Pergamon</b>, became centres of commerce, scholarship and culture.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After Attalus III of <b>Pergamon</b> bequeathed his kingdom to Rome in 133 BCE, Miletus came under Roman supremacy in the province of Asia, organised by 129 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
